OK — without color every series gets `DEFAULT_LINE_COLOR` (same color for all). So for multiple monitoring series I should provide distinct colors. These are chart-data palette values (canvas needs concrete colors), not UI chrome — providing a palette hex list is the documented usage for yagr (its examples pass hex colors). I'll define a small palette constant for monitoring series and let gravity-charts use its own built-in themed default palette for the sales chart.

Now write the code.

**Architecture:**

`src/viz-widget/`:
- `types.ts` — unified API
- `adapters.ts` — data mapping to engine configs
- `VizWidget.tsx` — single component
- `index.ts` — re-exports

`src/registerChartPlugins.ts` — settings.set at entry, imported by main.tsx before App.

`src/App.tsx` — demo: Card with header (Text + SegmentedRadioGroup toggle), VizWidget, generated sample data.

Unified API design:

```ts
export type VizWidgetMode = 'sales' | 'monitoring';

interface VizWidgetBaseProps {
  title?: string;
  height?: number;  // default 320
}

export interface SalesSeriesData {
  name: string;
  points: {t: number; value: number}[];
}

export interface MonitoringSeriesData {
  id: string;
  name: string;
  color?: string;
  values: (number | null)[];
}

export type VizWidgetProps = VizWidgetBaseProps &
  (
    | {mode: 'sales'; sales: {series: SalesSeriesData[]; yAxisTitle?: string}}
    | {mode: 'monitoring'; monitoring: {timeline: number[]; series: MonitoringSeriesData[]; yAxisTitle?: string}}
  );
```

VizWidget:
```tsx
export function VizWidget(props: VizWidgetProps) {
  const {title, height = 320} = props;
  return (
    <div className={b()} style={{height}}>
      {props.mode === 'sales' ? (
        <ChartKit type="gravity-charts" data={buildSalesChartData(props.sales)} />
      ) : (
        <ChartKit type="yagr" data={buildMonitoringWidgetData(props.monitoring)} />
      )}
    </div>
  );
